feat(mcp): a harness that ends a session ends what it started (#260)

Measured: five subagent runs sat Active for between three and nine hours after
the processes behind them were gone, and an operator closed them by hand. A
killed subagent fires no stop of its own, and the reaper's grace is twelve
hours by policy; shortening it kills working agents, which is what E9 exists to
say. The party that knew was the session that started them, and it said nothing
on its way out.

observe_session's stop gains `ends_descendants`, default OFF. Set, it walks the
parent edge downwards through the ledger's own ChildRuns and ends every
still-open run below this one through the shipped retire_agent, in process.

  - An ASSERTION, not an inference. Nothing reads a parent's retirement and
    concludes anything about a child's; the only thing that ends a descendant
    is a stop that carried the flag, at the moment it carried it (IP §3 E7).
  - A cascaded ending is byte-identical to any other `run_retired`. No event
    type, field, enum or source changes, and no idempotency key (ADR-0004) —
    migrations/0004's partial unique index is what holds it to one per run.
  - Lapsed and abandoned descendants are still open and are ended; only an
    already-retired one is skipped. Silence is not an ending (#258).
  - A stop still never blocks: a failed lookup, a failed retirement and an
    unwired deployment are all reported in `detail` and none refuses.

Driven by MCP-083 (descendants ended, to every depth), MCP-084 (a repeat
appends nothing) and MCP-085 (a partial failure is reported and non-fatal),
each watched failing first, and by OPS-056 in the shim self-test — a session
that starts a subagent, is killed, and asserts the ending only at its end.

cmd/innsegl/servewiring.go passes the parentage lookup: one additive field.
Without it the flag is inert and every stop ends exactly its own run.

